  "preamble": "Stressed open-mid [ɔ] breaks into the diphthong ue [we]. In Spanish, Latin short ŏ, pronounced [ɔ] in Classical Latin, became ue in stressed syllables, open or closed (bonus > bueno, porta > puerta); unstressed ŏ stayed o. Asturian has the same outcome (porta > puerta, focus > fueu), and Old French shows a parallel ue (novem > nuef, bovem > buef).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Diphthongization, Vowel Breaking",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Latin to Spanish",
      "languageFamily": "Ibero-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Latin bonus [ˈbɔ.nʊs] 'good'",
          "to": "Spanish bueno /ˈbweno/ [ˈbwe.no]"
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin porta [ˈpɔr.ta] 'gate'",
          "to": "Spanish puerta /ˈpweɾta/ 'door'",
          "note": "Unlike Italian, Spanish diphthongizes stressed ŏ in closed syllables as well."
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin rota [ˈrɔ.ta] 'wheel'",
          "to": "Spanish rueda /ˈrweda/"
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin mola [ˈmɔ.ɫa] 'millstone'",
          "to": "Spanish muela /ˈmwela/ 'molar'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin focus [ˈfɔ.kʊs] 'hearth'",
          "to": "Spanish fuego /ˈfweɡo/ 'fire'"
